      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Regulatory (CD4(+)CD25(+)) T cells have been shown to play an important role in the development of allergic diseases. This study aims to investigate CD4(+)CD25(+) T cells, Forkhead box P3 (FoxP3(+) cells), and T-helper 1/T-helper 2 (Th1/Th2) cytokines in newly diagnosed allergic rhinitis (AR) patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Altogether, 10 subjects with AR and 12 age-matched nonallergic healthy subjects were included in this study. CD4(+)CD25(+) T cells, FoxP3(+) T cells in peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) were evaluated by flow cytometry, and the Th1/Th2 cytokine levels were determined by cytometric bead array immunoassay in both PBMC supernatants and nasal lavage fluids."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The percentage of CD4(+)CD25(+) T cells were significantly higher, whereas the percentage of FoxP3(+) cells were lower in AR patients compared with healthy subjects. In PBMC culture supernatants, interleukin-10 (IL-10) levels were significantly lower (p = .012), whereas IL-4, IL-5, and tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α) levels in nasal lavage fluids were higher in AR patients compared with healthy subjects (p = .026, p = .015, p = .03, respectively)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Our findings indicate that decrease in CD4(+)CD25(+)FoxP3(+) T cell fraction and diminished levels of IL-10 are noteworthy without allergen stimulation in house dust mite AR patients."
        }
      ]},
